Kernel BTF for Android CPU accounting
====================================

The Android 16 timeInState.bpf on RMX2061 uses CO-RE relocations. The rooted
device baseline failed in libbpf before program loading:
  kernel BTF is missing at '/sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux'
  Error loading vmlinux BTF: -3

The default atoll configuration leaves BTF disabled because the build host
cannot install its additional tools. CPU time-in-state accounting therefore
remains unresolved. JMP32, GPU work-period reporting and the CPU touch-boost
fix do not require kernel BTF and remain enabled.

On a host with the prerequisites below, opt in with CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO=y,
CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_DWARF4=y and CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF=y. This tree already
exports BTF through kernel/bpf/sysfs_btf.c, but its in-kernel parser used
obsolete symbol names. Both now use the linker-defined __start_BTF and
__stop_BTF. Type/name limits match Linux v5.10's vmlinux-sized BTF limits.

Build prerequisites (Linux host):
- pahole v1.21 or newer (usually packaged as dwarves or pahole).
- The upstream Linux resolve_btfids host executable. Some distributions
  ship it under /usr/lib/linux-kbuild-*/tools/bpf/resolve_btfids/.
  Alternatively build tools/bpf/resolve_btfids in a Linux v5.10+ source tree.
  The Makefile checks PATH, /usr/lib/linux-kbuild-*, and /usr/src/linux-headers-*.
  For another location, supply make RESOLVE_BTFIDS=/path/to/resolve_btfids,
  or export RESOLVE_BTFIDS=/path/to/resolve_btfids before your build script.
  It must be a host binary, not an Android executable.

This kernel's bundled tools/lib/bpf predates the resolver's dependencies;
use the upstream tool instead of assuming an unbuilt in-tree binary exists.
If prepare-btf reports a missing resolver, inspect the build host first:
  cat /etc/os-release
  find /usr/lib /usr/src -type f -name resolve_btfids -executable 2>/dev/null
Package names vary by distribution. Installing bpftool alone does not supply
resolve_btfids. A build with BTF disabled avoids this host dependency but
does not fix the demonstrated CPU-accounting failure.
The prepare step checks both tools before compiling kernel objects. To
check manually with the same environment:
  PAHOLE=pahole RESOLVE_BTFIDS=/path/to/resolve_btfids sh scripts/btf-tools.sh

Unsupported newer BTF kinds (enum64, declaration/type tags) are disabled
when the installed pahole exposes those options. BTF generation failures
stop the build rather than silently producing a kernel without metadata.
Debug information increases build disk usage and compile time. The boot
image retains BTF; it does not retain the full DWARF sections.

Host prerequisite check:
  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bpf/test_btf_tools.py

After a full kernel build and flash (including the JMP32 commit):
  adb shell "su -c 'test -s /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux'"
  adb shell "su -c 'bpftool btf dump file /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux format raw'"
  adb shell "su -c 'find /sys/fs/bpf -iname \"*timeInState*\"'"
  adb shell "su -c 'dmesg | grep -i timeInState'"

Verify Android pins the CPU-accounting maps/programs and no longer logs a
timeInState.bpf load failure. Missing BTF was the first demonstrated blocker;
successful host checks do not prove all subsequent CO-RE/verifier checks pass.
Full kernel compilation and post-flash validation are still required.

References:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v5.10/include/uapi/linux/btf.h
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/tree/v5.10/tools/bpf/resolve_btfids
